### Zero Config TUIs

<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Zero-config TUIs are part of what makes `compose.mk` useful in [tool mode]({{_}}/tool-overview), especially as a quick option for interacting with third-party projects that you can't control and don't want to edit.  

Examples here all involve full-blown TUIs, i.e. multi-pane content with layouts that can be created with no code/config.  For something simpler that's still interactive, see also the [selector docs]({{_}}/selector).

#### Basic Examples 

<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

For a simple demo that's just exercising containerized `tmux`, geometry, and dispatching targets to panes.. try {{site.api_link('tux.demo',mkdocs)}}:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk tux.demo
```
{{macros.img_link("tui-tte.gif", mkdocs, "90%",class="cli_output")}}

Embedded TUI capapbilities are usually pulled/built just-in-time and on request, then cached afterwards. So besides doing some basic testing, this has the effect of "priming" the TUI for the other examples below.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

For a more involved example of dashboarding, try {{site.api_link('docker.commander',mkdocs)}}.  This demonstrates using other containers as TUI widgets- in this case we combine [lazydocker](https://github.com/jesseduffield/lazydocker), [moncho/dry](https://github.com/moncho/dry), and [imgrot](https://github.com/Robot-Wranglers/docker-imgrot).


```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk docker.commander  
```
{{macros.img_link("docker.commander.gif", mkdocs, "90%",class="cli_output")}}


#### Working with Compose-Files
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

**If your project has a docker-compose file,** the [selector docs]({{_}}/selector/#interactive-container-selection) describe how you can select a single container interactively and shell into it.  In a similar vein, you can also use the [`loadf` command]({{_}}/loadf) to **open several container shells at once.**

```bash {.cli_example}
# Opens 3 panes with container shells: 
# one for each service mentioned in the file
$ ./compose.mk loadf demos/data/docker-compose.yml
```
{{macros.img_link("loadf-1.png", mkdocs, "70%", class="cli_output")}}

Implicitly `loadf` is dynamically creating make-targets corresponding to the compose-services.  We can get more explicit by using `tux.open` as seen in the [Layout Section]({{_}}/embedded-tui/#layout-basics), and passing in services names or target names:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Equivalent to above, but more explicit and less magical.
$ layout=spiral ./compose.mk loadf demos/data/docker-compose.yml \
    tux.open.service_shells/debian,alpine,ubuntu

# Equivalent to above, but even more explicit
$ layout=spiral ./compose.mk loadf demos/data/docker-compose.yml \
    tux.open/debian.shell,alpine.shell,ubuntu.shell
```

#### Working with Makefiles
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

**If your project has a Makefile,** the [selector docs]({{_}}/selector/#interactive-target-selection) show how you can interactively select/run individual targets.  In a similar vein, **working with multiple targets and simple "dashboarding" across targets is also possible** with no special configuration.  This involves the {{site.api_link('include', mkdocs)}} target, which effectively simulates a `include compose.mk` in your project Makefile *without actually adding one.*

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

Consider the following vanilla Makefile, which does **not** include `compose.mk` itself, and simply defines fake `build`, `clean`, and `test` targets that do nothing.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/no-include.mk', github=github)}}

Using `include` by itself works in the way that you'd probably expect, but does nothing very interesting:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Roughly equivalent to `make -f demos/no-include.mk`
$ ./compose.mk \
    include/demos/no-include.mk 
```
```bash {.cli_output}
cleaning
building
testing
```

The interesting part is that using {{site.api_link('tux.open',mkdocs,arg='target_list')}} in the way that [we've already seen]({{_}}/embedded-tui/#layout-basics) *also* works how you'd expect here.  Thus you can use the command below to open each target in a separate pane:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk \
        include/demos/no-include.mk \
        tux.open/clean,build,test
```

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

This is neat so far.. but it's not that useful to run *clean/build/test* in parallel.

How about something more practical, like starting a TUI that runs the *clean/build* targets back-to-back, plus a separate pane that's running *test* in a loop?  That's doable with the workflow helpers in the [`flux.*`]({{_}}/api#api-flux) target namespace.  The command line for this starts to get complicated, so let's build it up one piece at a time. 

Running `clean` and `build` in one step:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk \
        include/demos/no-include.mk \
        flux.wrap/clean:build
```
```bash {.cli_output}
cleaning
building
```

Looping the test-target forever:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk \
        include/demos/no-include.mk \
        flux.loopf/test
```
```bash {.cli_output}
testing
testing
testing
^C
```

Putting it all together:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ layout=horizontal \
    ./compose.mk \
        include/demos/no-include.mk \
        tux.open/flux.loopf/test,flux.wrap/clean:build
```
{{macros.img_link("zero-config-tui.gif", mkdocs, "90%", class="cli_output")}}

#### Progress Bars
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Long-running fan-outs get a progress bar for free: any target built from the `@io.dirhandler` decorator (or `flux.each.dir`) streams one automatically.  Under the hood this is [`tux.progress`](https://github.com/mattvonrocketstein/tux.progress), a tiny static-binary renderer driven by newline-delimited JSON (`{index, total, output}`) on stdin.  It draws a charm-style bar on **stderr** while passing the stream through unchanged on **stdout**, runs from a `scratch` container (nothing lands on your host), and disables itself outside a TTY or in CI.

Anything emitting that contract can drive it directly:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ <producer emitting {index,total,output} JSON> | ./compose.mk tux.progress
```

#### Interactive REPL
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

!!! warning "Non-core"
    REPL support is **not** part of core `compose.mk`; it requires the non-core `repl` plugin (`tux.repl`), built on first-use.  See it in the [plugin index]({{_}}/plugins/overview/#plugin-index), and the [plugin overview]({{_}}/plugins/overview) for how plugins load.

Beyond dashboards, you can drop into an interactive **REPL**[^repl] over a project's targets with no setup.  With a file, it opens a shell over *that program's* target namespace; with no file, it's a plain shell over the core namespace:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk cmk repl                         # a shell over the core namespace
$ ./compose.mk cmk repl demos/cmk/overlay.cmk   # a shell over a program's targets
```

A program can also *default* to the REPL (so a bare `cmk run <file>` launches it) via the `repl` pragma; see the [compiler docs]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) for that and the underlying `tux.repl` harness.

#### Next Steps
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Expanding on this.. you could add the {{site.api_link('io.shell',mkdocs)}} target to the comma-separated list of `tux.open` arguments for an additional pane featuring an interactive shell.  Or you could add the {{site.api_link('io.env',mkdocs,arg='var_prefix')}} target to dump a list of relevant environment variables for this project.  You get the idea.

This is pretty useful, but the command-line is unwieldy, and a swiss-army knife CLI will only take you so far.  The next section looks at how we can achieve the same thing by editing files and avoiding one-liners.

